        optionFeedback: [`Bile duct atresia would lead to persistent obstruction and jaundice.`,`Congenital CMV infection may cause jaundice but usually is associated with other findings (such as sensorineural hearing loss, chorioretinitis, and other abnormalities).`,`The classic syndrome caused by mutations affecting a bile transporter is Dubin-Johnson syndrome. In this rare disease, there is conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (direct bilirubin), and the jaundice is usually mild and does not require treatment. `,`This child developed physiologic jaundice of the newborn, a common problem because of immaturity of the bilirubin conjugating system. Bilirubin, a breakdown product of heme, is conjugated in hepatocytes with glucuronic acid by the enzyme glucuronosyltransferase (UGT1), producing a water-soluble form that is readily excreted in the bile. Neonates often have low levels of this enzyme, leading to accumulation of unconjugated bilirubin. Mutations affecting the enzyme are the cause of hereditary diseases (Crigler–Najjar syndrome and Gilbert syndrome). `,`In hemolytic disease of the newborn, there may be jaundice because of excessive breakdown of red cells and heme, leading to increased production of bilirubin. The most common cause of hemolytic disease of the newborn is Rh incompatibility which rarely, if ever, gives rise to jaundice in the firstborn child.`],
